<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Florida Parishes</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> Parishes are eight parishes of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> lying east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi River</a> and north of Lake Pontchartrain. Formerly part of Spanish West Florida, the area rebelled as the Republic of West Florida in 1810, was annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> the same year and joined the state of Louisiana in 1812.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/florida-parishes.jpg" alt="Florida Parishes" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: getmahesh · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown_Baton_Rouge.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Florida Parishes, also called the Northshore or Northlake region, stretch from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> state line on their eastern and northern borders to the Mississippi River in the west and Lake Pontchartrain in the south. They cover 4,685.184 square miles (12,134.57 km2), or 10.755% of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>'s land area. Unlike most of the state, the region was not part of the 1803 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana-purchase">Louisiana Purchase</a>, which applied to territory west of the Mississippi River. Its climate is humid subtropical and prone to hurricanes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area was first claimed by French colonists as part of French Louisiana. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-and-indian-war">French and Indian War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> ceded the eastern half of Louisiana, excluding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1763">1763 Treaty of Paris</a>. Britain divided its new Gulf coast lands at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apalachicola-river">Apalachicola River</a> into the provinces of East and West Florida. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a> it ceded them back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and uncertainty over their borders led to the West Florida Controversy with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><p>Pinckney's Treaty of 1795 settled the first dispute, but the sale of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> in 1803 raised new questions, since the Third Treaty of San Ildefonso and the Treaty of Aranjuez had not included Spanish West Florida. American and British settlers west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pearl-river">Pearl River</a>, aggrieved by the provincial Spanish government, elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulwar-skipwith">Fulwar Skipwith</a> governor of their republic. After its annexation four parishes were established in 1810: East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baton-rouge">Baton Rouge</a>, Feliciana, St. Helena and St. Tammany. Washington Parish followed in 1819, East and West Feliciana in 1824 when Feliciana was divided, Livingston in 1832 and Tangipahoa in 1869, during Reconstruction.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The most populous urban area is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baton-rouge">Baton Rouge</a> metropolitan area, and St. Tammany Parish belongs to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a> combined statistical area; the region counted 1,088,014 people in 2020. The Roman Catholic Church is its largest denomination and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baptists">Baptists</a> the second. In 1990 the legislature named it the Republic of West Florida Historic Region, Interstate 12 has been the Republic of West Florida Parkway since 1993, and the republic's flag flies on many public buildings.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Florida Parishes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Most of it became the <b>Florida Parishes</b> of Louisiana; the area east of the Pearl River later went to Mississippi and Alabama.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-west-florida">Republic of West Florida</a></li></ul></section>
